**Implementation of `rename` function in `/Users/tijs/projects/local-model-bench/runner/runs/tmpwk8wvq52/run/rust/src/store.rs`:**

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let new_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id));

    fs::rename(&old_path, &new_path)?;

    let content = fs::read_to_string(&new_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();

    if parts.len() == 4 {
        let pinned_flag = if parts[2] == "1" { "1" } else { "0" };
        let new_content = format!(
            "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
            new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned_flag, SEP, parts[3]
        );
        fs::write(&new_path, new_content)?;
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

The function:
1. Constructs the old and new file paths (based on the note filename convention `{id}.note`)
2. Renames the file on disk using `fs::rename`
3. Reads the file content and parses it using the same separator (`SEP = "\n---\n"`)
4. Creates new content with the new_id and new_title, preserving body, tags, and pinned state
5. Writes the updated content back to the file

The tests verify:
- The note is correctly renamed with the new id and title
- Body, tags, and pinned state are preserved
- The old file no longer exists on disk
- The new file exists with correct content
- Error handling for non-existent notes


